[Chapter "Key ideas: White's plan"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "ideas"]

{One slow plan works against almost every Black setup in this course. Finish developing behind the pawns on c3, d3 and e4, then open the center with the d-pawn when your pieces are ready. Each board shows one part of the plan.}
*

[Chapter "The knight's trip to g3"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Diagram "9...h6"]

{The pawn on c3 takes the c3 square from the queen's knight, so it travels b1-d2-f1-g3 instead. From g3 it eyes f5 and h5, near Black's king. h3, already played, keeps Black's bishop and knight off g4 while the knight makes the trip.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 d6 6. O-O O-O 7. Re1 a6 8. Bb3 Ba7 9. h3 h6 {[%cal Gb1d2,Gd2f1,Gf1g3] The idea starts from this position.} 10. Nbd2 {First stop: d2.} Re8 11. Nf1 {The rook on e1 still guards e4, so the knight can leave d2.} Be6 {Black offers to trade White's Italian bishop.} 12. Bc2 {[%cal Gf1g3] Keep the bishop. Next comes Ng3, and the plan is complete.} *

[Chapter "The d4 break, when you are ready"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Diagram "12...a5"]

{With every piece developed, White opens the center with d4. Here Black has spent a move on ...a5 instead of freeing the game with ...d5, so the break comes at once.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 d6 6. O-O O-O 7. Re1 a6 8. Bb3 Ba7 9. h3 h6 10. Nbd2 Re8 11. Nf1 Be6 12. Bc2 a5 {[%cal Gd3d4] [%csl Ye5] The idea starts from this position.} 13. d4 {Hits e5. The rook on e1 already guards e4.} exd4 14. Nxd4 {[%csl Gd4,Ge4] White's pieces are active and the center is open. Stockfish: +0.5, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Against the ...Bg4 pin"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Diagram "11...h6"]

{When Black pins the knight on f3, ask the bishop with h3 and guard f3 with Nbd2. With the pawn on c3, Black can never add ...Nd4 to attack the pinned knight again. Then the knights hunt the bishop.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 d6 6. O-O Bg4 7. h3 Bh5 8. Nbd2 O-O 9. Re1 a6 10. Bb3 Ba7 11. Nf1 h6 {[%cal Gf1g3,Gg3h5] The idea starts from this position.} 12. Ng3 {Attacks the bishop on h5.} Bg6 13. Nh4 {[%cal Gh4g6] Hits the bishop again.} Bh7 {Black's most common reply.} 14. Nhf5 {[%csl Gf5] The knight lands on f5, near Black's king. Stockfish rates White slightly better.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Pawn breaks"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Pawn breaks decide this opening. White's is the d-pawn push to d4 (see White's plan). Black's is the d-pawn push to d5, and it only works once Black has finished developing.}
*

[Chapter "Black's ...d5, played in time"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "12.Bc2"]

{Black has developed every piece and the rook stands on e8, so ...d5 is safe here. After the trade on d5 the center opens and the game is level.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 d6 6. O-O O-O 7. Re1 a6 8. Bb3 Ba7 9. h3 h6 10. Nbd2 Re8 11. Nf1 Be6 12. Bc2 {[%cal Gd6d5] The idea starts from this position.} 12... d5 {The freeing break.} 13. exd5 Bxd5 14. Ng3 {[%cal Gf3h4,Gg3f5] White turns to the kingside: Nh4 and Nf5. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Punishing an early ...d5"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Diagram "6.O-O"]

{Here Black plays ...d5 before finishing development. Trade on d5 and hit e5 at once.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 O-O 6. O-O {[%cal Gd7d5] The idea starts from this position.} 6... d5 {Black frees the game before every piece is out.} 7. exd5 Nxd5 8. Re1 {[%cal Ge1e5,Gf3e5] e5 is attacked twice and defended once. Black must spend a move guarding it while White keeps developing. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Typical tactics"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Five patterns come up again and again in the Italian. Two more, the Fishing Pole and Légal's mate, have their own trap chapters.}
*

[Chapter "f7 is the target"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Diagram "4...Nf6"]

{Black has shut in the f8 bishop with ...d6 and played ...Nf6, so only the king guards f7. Ng5 adds a second attacker.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 d6 4. Nc3 Nf6? {[%cal Gc4f7] [%csl Rf7] The idea starts from this position.} 5. Ng5 {[%cal Gg5f7,Gc4f7] Two attackers, one defender.} d5 {Black has to block the bishop's line.} 6. exd5 {White wins a pawn. Stockfish: +1.1, clearly better for White.} *

[Chapter "The Qb3 battery"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Diagram "5...d5"]

{Queen and bishop on the a2-g8 diagonal hit d5 and f7 together. It punishes ...d5 played too early.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 d5?! {[%cal Gc4d5,Gd1b3] The idea starts from this position.} 6. exd5 Nxd5 7. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3d5,Gb3f7] Hits d5 and f7 together. Black has no comfortable way to guard both. Stockfish: +0.9, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "The pinned f7 pawn"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Diagram "13.Nh4"]

{White's bishop on b3 pins the f7 pawn to Black's king, and Black's h-pawn has gone to h6. So nothing can take back on g6.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 d6 6. O-O Bg4 7. h3 Bh5 8. Nbd2 O-O 9. Re1 a6 10. Bb3 Ba7 11. Nf1 h6 12. Ng3 Bg6 13. Nh4 {[%cal Gb3g8,Gh4g6] The idea starts from this position.} 13... Re8? {Leaves the bishop on g6.} 14. Nxg6 {Wins the bishop: neither the f-pawn nor the h-pawn can recapture.} *

[Chapter "Keep the Italian bishop"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Diagram "7...Na5"]

{Black's knight attacks the bishop on c4. Don't let it trade off the Italian bishop: drop it back to a safe square.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. d3 Be7 5. O-O O-O 6. Re1 d6 7. c3 Na5 {[%cal Ra5c4] The idea starts from this position.} 8. Bb5 {Avoids the trade.} a6 9. Ba4 b5 10. Bc2 {[%cal Gc2h7] Safe on c2. Once d4 is played, it points at Black's king.} *

[Chapter "The Bg5 pin, only after castling"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Diagram "6...O-O"]

{Pin the knight only once Black has castled. Against a king still on e8, ...h6 and ...g5 cost Black nothing. Here Black has castled, so ...g5 loosens the king.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 d6 6. O-O O-O {[%cal Gg5f6] The idea starts from this position.} 7. Bg5 h6 8. Bh4 g5 9. Bg3 {Retreat. The knight sacrifice 9.Nxg5? hxg5 10.Bxg5 looks tempting, but Stockfish rates it about a pawn and a quarter worse for White.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Black's plan"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Match White's patience, then choose the right moment to push the d-pawn to d5. Each board shows one step of the setup.}
*

[Chapter "...a6 and ...Ba7 keep the bishop"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "6.O-O"]

{White will play d4 or b4 to chase the bishop on c5. Make room for it on a7 first.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 d6 6. O-O {[%cal Gc5a7] The idea starts from this position.} 6... a6 {Prepares ...Ba7.} 7. a4 {Stops ...b5.} Ba7 {[%cal Ga7f2] On a7 the bishop is safe from d4 and b4 and still aims at f2.} *

[Chapter "...h6: no Bg5, no Ng5"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "9.h3"]

{White's bishop and knight both want the g5 square. One pawn move takes it from them.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 d6 6. O-O a6 7. a4 Ba7 8. Re1 O-O 9. h3 {[%csl Yg5] The idea starts from this position.} 9... h6 {[%csl Rg5] No Bg5 pin, and no Ng5 jump toward f7.} 10. Nbd2 Re8 {Supports e5 and gets ready for ...Be6 and ...d5.} *

[Chapter "...Be6 trades White's best bishop"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "11.Nf1"]

{White's bishop on c4 is its best attacking piece. Offer to trade it with ...Be6. Prefer ...Be6 to ...Bg4: with White's pawn on c3, Black can never add ...Nd4, so a pin on f3 does little.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 d6 6. O-O a6 7. a4 Ba7 8. Re1 O-O 9. h3 h6 10. Nbd2 Re8 11. Nf1 {[%cal Gc8e6] The idea starts from this position.} 11... Be6 12. Bxe6 Rxe6 {The rook takes back, and Black's pawns stay undoubled.} *

[Chapter "...d5, when ready"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "13.Ng3"]

{Every piece is out and the rook supports e5. Now the freeing break is safe.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 d6 6. O-O a6 7. a4 Ba7 8. Re1 O-O 9. h3 h6 10. Nbd2 Re8 11. Nf1 Be6 12. Bxe6 Rxe6 13. Ng3 {[%cal Gd6d5] The idea starts from this position.} 13... d5 {Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: The isolated d-pawn"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "ideas"]

{When White opens the center early, you free your game by pushing your d-pawn to d5 (see the Center attack chapter). The board shows the pawn structure that follows. It decides the plans.}
*

[Chapter "A pawn with no neighbors"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "12...c6"]

{White's d-pawn is isolated: no White pawn stands on the c- or e-file beside it, so no pawn can ever guard it. No White pawn can attack d5 either, so your knight is safe there. Trades help you: the fewer pieces are left, the weaker the lone pawn becomes.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d4 exd4 6. cxd4 Bb4+ 7. Bd2 Bxd2+ 8. Nbxd2 d5 9. exd5 Nxd5 10. Qb3 Nce7 11. O-O O-O 12. Rfe1 c6 {[%csl Rd4,Gd5] The idea starts from this position.} 13. Ne4 {White's chances come early, from active pieces.} a5 14. Qa3 Qc7 {[%csl Gd5] Black finishes developing and keeps the knight on d5. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Tricks to avoid"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Some tempting ideas don't work against a calm opponent. Each board shows one and how White answers it.}
*

[Chapter "The early ...Ng4"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Diagram "5.O-O"]

{Attacking f2 with a lone knight loses time. Here White even has a tactic against it.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. d3 Nf6 5. O-O {[%cal Rf6g4,Rg4f2] The idea starts from this position.} 5... Ng4? {[%engine ok] Eyes f2, but leaves f7 short of defenders.} 6. Bxf7+ Kxf7 7. Ng5+ Kg8 8. Qxg4 {White has won a pawn and Black's king has lost its shelter. Stockfish: +1.1, clearly better for White.} *

[Chapter "The early ...d5"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Diagram "5.d3"]

{Played before you have developed, ...d5 lets White's queen join the bishop against d5 and f7. Prepare it with ...Re8 and ...Be6 first.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 {[%cal Rd7d5] The idea starts from this position.} 5... d5?! {[%engine ok] Too early.} 6. exd5 Nxd5 7. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3d5,Gb3f7] Hits d5 and f7 together. Black has no comfortable way to guard both. Stockfish: +0.9, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "...g5, but not ...h5"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "8.Bg3"]

{Breaking the Bg5 pin with ...h6 and ...g5 is fine. But the g5 pawn needs the h-pawn's support, so don't push ...h5. Play 8...a6 and ...Ba7 instead.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 d6 6. Bg5 h6 7. Bh4 g5 8. Bg3 {[%csl Yg5] The idea starts from this position.} 8... h5? {[%engine ok] Too ambitious: now nothing guards g5.} 9. Nxg5 {[%cal Gf3g5] White grabs the pawn, and Black's kingside is broken. Stockfish: +2.1, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Main line: 3...Bc5"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Classical Variation, Giuoco Pianissimo"]
[ECO "C54"]

{3...Bc5 is the Giuoco Piano: Black copies White and puts the bishop on its best diagonal, aimed at f2. We answer with the Giuoco Pianissimo, a slow build-up that keeps every option open.}
1. e4 {Takes the center and opens lines for the bishop and queen.} 1... e5 2. Nf3 {Develops with an attack on e5.} 2... Nc6 {Defends e5.} 3. Bc4 {[%cal Gc4f7] The Italian bishop targets f7, the weakest point near Black's king, and controls d5.} 3... Bc5 {Black's bishop mirrors ours. From c5 it controls d4 and eyes f2.} 4. c3 {[%cal Gc3d4] The key move of the Italian. c3 supports a later d4, so White can build a pawn center that Black's pieces can't easily attack. It also frees c2 as a retreat square for the bishop. The cost is that the knight can no longer go to c3.} 4... Nf6 {Counterattacks e4 at once, before White is ready for d4.} 5. d3 {Guards e4 calmly. The older 5.d4 opens the game at once and leads to sharp theory in which Black equalizes with an early ...d5. With 5.d3 we keep d4 in reserve and play it later, when it gains more.} 5... d6 {Supports e5 and opens the diagonal for the c8 bishop. Both sides now build slowly.} 6. O-O O-O {Black's other sixth moves, 6...a6 and the pin 6...Bg4, are under Advanced variations.} 7. Re1 {Protects e4 a second time and gets ready for the center to open after d4.} 7... a6 {Gives the bishop a retreat on a7, so d4 or b4 won't gain time against it. It also prepares ...b5.} 8. Bb3 {Steps back before ...b5 or ...Na5 can hit the bishop. On b3 it still aims at f7.} 8... Ba7 9. h3 {[%csl Yg4] Takes g4 from Black's bishop and knight: no pin on f3 and no ...Ng4 raid. Now the knight on f3 and the bishop on c1 can move freely.} 9... h6 {Black mirrors the idea and takes g5 from our bishop and knight.} 10. Nbd2 Re8 11. Nf1 {[%cal Gf1g3] The classic Italian knight route, b1-d2-f1-g3. The knight couldn't use c3, so it travels to the kingside, where it eyes f5 and h5.} 11... Be6 {Offers to trade off our best bishop.} 12. Bc2 {Keeps the bishop. On c2 it waits behind the d3 pawn and points at Black's king once d4 is played.} 12... d5 {The freeing break. Black is fully developed, so ...d5 is safe now.} 13. exd5 Bxd5 14. Ng3 {[%cal Gg3f5,Gf3h4] Development is complete. Next comes Nh4 and Nf5, or the d4 break, depending on Black's setup.} *

[Chapter "Two Knights: 3...Nf6"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Two Knights Defense, Modern Bishop's Opening"]
[ECO "C55"]

{3...Nf6 is the Two Knights Defense. It attacks e4 at once and invites 4.Ng5, the sharp territory of the Fried Liver Attack. We decline with 4.d3 and keep our usual setup.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. d3 {Protects e4. 4.Ng5 attacks f7 at once but leads to heavy theory after 4...d5. 4.d3 keeps the game on familiar ground and still allows c3 and d4 later.} 4... Be7 {Solid. On e7 the bishop is safe from d4 and b4, and Black prepares to castle.} 5. O-O O-O 6. Re1 d6 7. c3 {Prepares d4 and opens the c2 square for the bishop.} 7... Na5 {Black hunts our bishop. A knight on the edge is worth it if it wins the Italian bishop.} 8. Bb5 {Avoids the trade. The bishop will drop back to a4 and then c2.} 8... a6 9. Ba4 b5 10. Bc2 {Safe on c2. Once d4 is played, it points at Black's king.} 10... c5 {Black gains queenside space, Ruy Lopez style.} 11. Nbd2 Nc6 12. Nf1 {The knight goes to g3 next, and d4 comes when we are ready.} *

[Chapter "Castling first: 4.O-O"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Giuoco Piano"]
[ECO "C50"]

{White castles before choosing c3, d3 or Nc3. This is White's most common fourth move, about one game in four. Play 4...Nf6 and the same setup as always: ...d6, ...a6 and ...Ba7. Most games then reach the main line by another move order. The one new idea is 5.d4, where you take on d4 with the bishop: see Castling first: White's fifth moves, under Advanced variations.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. O-O {Castles first and keeps c3, d3 and Nc3 open.} 4... Nf6 {Hits e4, as in every line of this course.} 5. d3 {The most common reply, about three games in ten. It guards e4. This is the same position as 4.d3 Nf6 5.O-O.} 5... d6 6. h3 {The most common move, about four games in ten. It takes g4 away from Black's bishop and knight. 6.c3 leads straight back to the main line.} 6... a6 {Makes room for ...Ba7, as in the main line.} 7. c3 Ba7 {The main-line setup, reached by a different move order. Castle next, then ...h6.} *

[Chapter "Center attack: 5.d4"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Classical Variation, Center Attack"]
[ECO "C54"]

{White opens the center at once. This is the old main line that Greco analyzed around 1620. Three moves do the work: take on d4, check on b4, and free your game with ...d5.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d4 {Strikes in the center while our bishop on c5 is a target.} 5... exd4 {Take, so White doesn't get a big center for free.} 6. cxd4 {This is the Traditional Line of the Greco Gambit. 6.e5 is under Advanced variations.} 6... Bb4+ {Gains time before White can castle. White usually blocks with a piece.} 7. Bd2 {The Møller Attack, 7.Nc3, is under Advanced variations.} 7... Bxd2+ 8. Nbxd2 d5! {[%cal Gd7d5] The key freeing move. It challenges e4 and opens the diagonal for the c8 bishop.} 9. exd5 Nxd5 10. Qb3 {Lines up behind the bishop against d5 and also eyes b7.} 10... Nce7 {Defends d5 with a second knight.} 11. O-O O-O 12. Rfe1 c6 {Rock solid. White's d4 pawn is isolated and can become a target.} *

[Chapter "Quiet Variation: 4.d3"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Giuoco Pianissimo"]
[ECO "C50"]

{4.d3 guards e4 at once, about one game in seven. Play 4...Nf6 and your usual setup. Against an early Bg5 pin, push it back with ...h6: you haven't castled yet, so ...g5 is safe if the bishop stays on the diagonal. See Quiet Variation: 5.Bg5, under Advanced variations.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. d3 {Guards e4 and keeps c3 for later.} 4... Nf6 5. O-O {The most common move, about one game in four. It is the same position as 4.O-O Nf6 5.d3.} 5... d6 6. c3 {6.h3 a6 7.c3 Ba7 is the same as the 4.O-O chapter.} 6... a6 {Back in the main line by a different move order.} *

[Chapter "Main line: 4.c3 Nf6 5.d3"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Classical Variation, Giuoco Pianissimo"]
[ECO "C54"]

{The most common setup at club level. Both sides build slowly, and small details decide who gets the useful pawn breaks. White's other sixth moves are under Advanced variations; the plan against them hardly changes.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 {[%cal Gc5f2] Develops to the best square: the bishop controls d4 and eyes f2.} 4. c3 {White prepares d4, which would hit our bishop and build a big center.} 4... Nf6 {Hits e4 at once, before White can play d4. White usually guards e4 with d3 or pushes d4 at once.} 5. d3 {The quiet Giuoco Pianissimo: White guards e4 and saves d4 for later.} 5... d6 {Supports e5 and opens the diagonal for the light-squared bishop. With e5 solid, White's d4 break will be less effective.} 6. O-O a6 {Makes room for ...Ba7 so the bishop survives d4 and b4.} 7. a4 {Stops ...b5, which would gain space and chase White's bishop.} 7... Ba7 8. Re1 O-O 9. h3 h6 {[%csl Yg5] Takes g5 away from White's bishop and knight, so there is no pin on f6 and no Ng5.} 10. Nbd2 Re8 {Supports e5 and prepares ...Be6 and ...d5.} 11. Nf1 {White's knight heads for g3.} 11... Be6 {Challenges White's best bishop.} 12. Bxe6 Rxe6 {The rook recaptures, and Black's pawns stay undoubled.} 13. Ng3 {Balanced. Black plans ...d5, or ...Qd7 and ...Rae8 first.} *

[Chapter "Evans Gambit: 4.b4"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Evans Gambit"]
[ECO "C51"]

{The Evans Gambit was a favorite of 19th-century attackers; Anderssen's famous Evergreen Game of 1852 began with it. White gives a pawn to gain time for c3 and d4. Take it, retreat to a5, and give the pawn back with ...d5 at the right moment.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. b4 Bxb4 {Accept: the pawn is free for now.}
5. c3 {Gains time on the bishop and prepares d4.} Ba5 {The bishop stays active and out of reach of White's d-pawn.}
6. d4
6... exd4 {This is the Pierce Defense.} 7. O-O Nge7 {This is the Mieses Defense. Develops and prepares ...d5.}
8. cxd4 d5! {[%cal Gd7d5] Gives the pawn back to free the position and blunt White's bishop.}
9. exd5 Nxd5 {Black's pieces are active. Castle next.} *

[Chapter "Italian Four Knights: 4.Nc3"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Giuoco Piano"]
[ECO "C50"]

{4.Nc3 develops quickly but gives up White's c3 and d4 plan, about one game in eleven. Play 4...Nf6, ...d6, ...a6 and ...Ba7. Against the pin Bg5, ask the bishop with ...h6 and break the pin with ...g5 before you castle. The fork trick 5.Nxe5? does not work here: see the trap chapter.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. Nc3 Nf6 5. d3 {About half of White players.} 5... d6 6. h3 {Takes g4 away from Black's bishop and knight.} 6... a6 {Prepares ...Ba7 as usual.} 7. a3 Ba7 8. O-O O-O {Both sides have castled. Next ...h6 and ...Be6.} *

[Chapter "Two Knights: 4...Bc5"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "4...Bc5"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Two Knights Defense, Modern Bishop's Opening"]
[ECO "C55"]

{4...Bc5, Black's main alternative after 4.d3, goes back to the main line with 5.c3.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. d3 Bc5 5. c3 {Transposes to the main line.} *

[Chapter "Other third moves: 3...Be7 and 3...h6"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "3...Be7"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Hungarian Defense"]
[ECO "C50"]

{Two quieter third moves: the Hungarian Defense 3...Be7, and 3...h6. Against both, the same setup works.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Be7 {The Hungarian Defense: solid but passive. It covers g5 against Ng5 ideas, but the bishop does less on e7 than it would on c5.}
(3... h6 {Prevents Ng5 and Bg5 but spends a move on a pawn. The usual setup is fine against it.} 4. d3 Nf6 5. c3 d6 6. O-O g6 7. Re1 Bg7 8. Nbd2 O-O 9. Nf1 {Our usual plan: Ng3, h3, then d4.})
4. d3 Nf6 5. O-O O-O 6. Re1 d6 7. c3 {The same structure as the Two Knights chapter.} *

[Chapter "Giuoco Piano: 4...d6"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "4...d6"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Classical Variation, Giuoco Pianissimo"]
[ECO "C54"]

{4...d6 is solid but doesn't attack e4. Keep the usual setup: after 5.d3 Nf6 the game is back in the main line.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 d6 {Solid, but it doesn't attack e4. 5.d4 is a good alternative here; we keep our usual setup.} 5. d3 Nf6 {Back in the main line by a different move order.} *

[Chapter "Giuoco Piano: 5...O-O"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "5...O-O"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Classical Variation, Giuoco Pianissimo"]
[ECO "C54"]

{Black castles before ...d6 and then strikes with ...d5 too early. Trade on d5 and hit e5 with Re1.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 O-O 6. O-O d5 {The freeing break Black wants in every Italian, played here before Black has finished developing.} 7. exd5 Nxd5 8. Re1 {[%cal Ge1e5,Gf3e5] Now e5 is attacked twice and defended once. Black must spend a move defending it while we keep developing.} *

[Chapter "Castling first: White's fifth moves"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "5.c3"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Giuoco Piano"]
[ECO "C50"]

{White's other fifth moves after 4.O-O Nf6. Against 5.d4, take with the bishop: 5...Bxd4. Against the others, play ...d6 and your usual setup.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. O-O Nf6 5. c3 (5. d4 {Opens the center at once, about one game in five.} Bxd4 {[%cal Gc5d4] Take with the bishop. Stockfish prefers it to 5...exd4, which leads to the sharp Max Lange Attack.} 6. Nxd4 Nxd4 {Black's knight now stands well on d4.} 7. f4 {White wants to open the f-file against e5 and f7.} (7. Bg5 {Pins the knight on f6, as often as 7.f4.} h6 8. Bh4 d6 9. f4 Bg4! {[%cal Gg4d1] Hits the queen. If White takes on f6, Black takes the queen too.} 10. Bxf6 Bxd1 11. Bxd8 Rxd8 12. Rxd1 Nxc2 {The knight attacks the rook on a1.} 13. Nc3 Nxa1 14. Rxa1 exf4 {Black has two rooks and eight pawns against a rook, bishop, knight and five pawns. Stockfish rates it about equal.}) 7... d6 {Guards e5, so fxe5 is met by ...dxe5.} 8. fxe5 dxe5 9. Bg5 Qe7 {Black's knight on d4 is strong, and Black is fine.}) 5... d6 {[%engine ok] Back in the main line: this is 4.c3 Nf6 5.O-O d6. Stockfish prefers 5...Nxe4, but the game then turns sharp.} *

[Chapter "Pianissimo: White's sixth moves"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "6.Nbd2"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Classical Variation, Giuoco Pianissimo"]
[ECO "C54"]

{White's other sixth moves in the Pianissimo, and 6.d3 after 5.O-O. The plan stays the same: ...a6 and ...Ba7 so the bishop is safe from b4 and d4, then castle and ...h6.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3
(5. O-O d6 6. d3 a6 {Back in the main line by a different move order.})
5... d6
6. Nbd2 {The knight heads for f1 and g3, as in White's usual plan.}
(6. a4 {Stops ...b5 and gains space on the queenside.} a6 {Same plan: make room for ...Ba7, where the bishop is safe from b4 and d4.} 7. O-O Ba7 {Back in the main line by a different move order.})
(6. Bb3 a6 7. Nbd2 Ba7 8. Nf1 O-O 9. Ng3 h6)
(6. Bg5 {Premature. Black hasn't castled, so the pin can be broken at no risk.} h6 7. Bh4 g5 8. Bg3 a6 {Calm. Now the pawns on g5 and h6 keep White's bishop and knight off g5.} (8... h5? {Too ambitious.} 9. Nxg5! {[%cal Gf3g5] With the h-pawn gone from h6, nothing guards g5. White grabs it, and Black's kingside is broken: Stockfish +2.1, winning for White.}) 9. O-O Ba7)
6... a6 7. O-O Ba7 8. Re1 O-O 9. Nf1 h6 {Takes g5 away from White's pieces.} 10. h3 Re8 *

[Chapter "Pianissimo: 6...a6 and 6...Bg4"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "6...Bg4"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Classical Variation, Giuoco Pianissimo"]
[ECO "C54"]

{Black's other sixth moves in the Pianissimo. Against 6...a6, gain queenside space with a4 and follow the usual plan. Against the pin 6...Bg4, ask the bishop with h3, guard f3 with Nbd2, and chase the bishop with Ng3 and Nh4.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 d6 6. O-O Bg4 {The tempting pin. Here it does little: with our pawn on c3, Black can never add ...Nd4 to attack the pinned knight again.}
(6... a6 7. a4 {Gains queenside space and stops ...b5, which would chase our bishop.} Ba7 8. Re1 O-O 9. h3 h6 10. Nbd2 Re8 11. Nf1 {Same setup as the main line: the knight heads for g3.})
7. h3 {Asks the bishop to decide.} Bh5 {Keeps the pin.} 8. Nbd2 {Guards f3 once more, so the pin is harmless.} O-O 9. Re1 a6 10. Bb3 {Steps back before ...Na5 can trade it off.} Ba7 11. Nf1 h6 12. Ng3 {[%cal Gg3h5] The knight arrives with tempo on the bishop.} Bg6 13. Nh4 {[%cal Gh4g6,Gb3g8] Now Nxg6 is a real threat. Our bishop pins the f7 pawn, and after ...h6 the h-pawn can't recapture either.} Bh7 {The most common reply: the bishop steps out of the knight's reach.} (13... Kh8 {Unpins f7, so Nxg6 can now be met by ...fxg6.}) (13... Re8? 14. Nxg6 {Wins the bishop: neither pawn can recapture.}) 14. Nhf5 {[%cal Gg3f5] The knight lands on f5, near Black's king, and the other knight backs it up. Stockfish rates White slightly better.} Bxf5 15. Nxf5 {Recapture with the knight, not the pawn: the knight on f5 is strong, and White keeps the two bishops.} *

[Chapter "Quiet Variation: 5.Bg5"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "5.Bg5"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Giuoco Pianissimo"]
[ECO "C50"]

{After 4.d3 Nf6, White pins the knight with 5.Bg5 before Black has castled. Push the bishop back with ...h6, and break the pin with ...g5 before you castle.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. d3 Nf6 5. Bg5 {Pins the knight before Black has castled.} h6 {Asks the bishop at once.} 6. Bh4 d6 {Calm. If the pin gets annoying, ...g5 breaks it. With Black's king still on e8, the pawn move costs nothing.} 7. h3 a6 8. c3 g5 {[%cal Gg5h4] Now the bishop must retreat or be given up.} 9. Bg3 Ba7 {The pin is gone and Black's usual setup is ready. Stockfish slightly prefers Black.} *

[Chapter "Center attack: 7.Nc3 and 6.e5"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "7.Nc3"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Classical Variation, Center Attack"]
[ECO "C54"]

{The sharp tries in the Center attack. Against the Møller Attack, 7.Nc3, take the e4 pawn: the pin on c3 makes it safe. Against 6.e5, counterattack with ...d5.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d4 exd4
6. cxd4 {This is the Traditional Line of the Greco Gambit.}
(6. e5 {This is the Modern Line of the Greco Gambit. Kicks the knight.} d5! {[%cal Gd5c4] Counterattacks the bishop instead of retreating. This is Black's only good reply.} 7. Bb5 Ne4 {A strong central knight.} 8. cxd4 Bb4+ {This is the Anderssen Variation of the Greco Gambit.} 9. Bd2 Nxd2 10. Nbxd2 O-O {Black has castled and stands well.})
6... Bb4+ 7. Nc3 {This is Greco's Attack.} Nxe4 {Take the center pawn. The pin on c3 makes this safe: the knight can't recapture.} 8. O-O Bxc3 {This is the Main Line of the Greco Gambit.} (8... Nxc3 {This is the Greco Variation of the Greco Gambit. Playable only with 9.bxc3 d5!} 9. bxc3 Bxc3? {Greedy.} 10. Ba3! {[%cal Ga3f8] This is the Aitken Variation. The bishop stops Black from castling, and White's attack wins: Stockfish rates it about +4.8.}) 9. d5 {This is the Møller Attack, also called the Moeller-Therkatz Attack. The Møller Attack: White gives a pawn for fast development.} (9. bxc3 d5! {The best answer. It hits the bishop and supports the knight, and Black keeps the extra pawn.} 10. Bd3 O-O 11. Qc2 Re8 12. Re1 Bf5 {The knight on e4 is rock solid.}) 9... Bf6 {The bishop drops back to its best defensive square.} 10. Re1 Ne7 11. Rxe4 d6 {Play ...d6 before castling, so the c8 bishop can come out.} 12. Bg5 Bxg5 13. Nxg5 h6! {[%engine ok] Kick the knight first. Stockfish at depth 26 rates it best. 13...O-O allows 14.Nxh7! Kxh7 15.Qh5+, and White draws by perpetual check.} 14. Qa4+ Bd7 15. Qb3 O-O {Black has castled and keeps the extra pawn. Stockfish rates it equal.} *

[Chapter "Paris Defense: 3...d6"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Paris Defense"]
[ECO "C50"]

{3...d6 supports e5 solidly but blocks Black's own bishop on f8. That leaves f7 with fewer defenders, and we aim at it at once.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 d6
4. Nc3 {Develops and adds control of d5. With Black's f8 bishop shut in, quick piece play pays off, and 4.Nc3 sets up Légal's trap if Black pins with ...Bg4 (see the trap chapter).} Nf6? {Natural, but now f7 has only the king to defend it.}
5. Ng5! {[%cal Gg5f7,Gc4f7] Two attackers on f7 and one defender. Black has to block the bishop with ...d5, and that costs a pawn.} d5
6. exd5 Na5 {Attacks the bishop. This is Black's best try to win the pawn back.}
(6... Nxd5? 7. Nxf7! {The Fried Liver sacrifice.} Kxf7 8. Bxd5+ {Wins back the piece with check.} Ke8 9. Bxc6+ bxc6 10. Qh5+ Kd7 11. Qf5+ Ke8 12. Qxe5+ Qe7 {White has two extra pawns, and Black's king is stuck in the center.})
7. Bb5+ {Check first, gaining time. After ...c6 and dxc6 the bishop stays active.} c6
8. dxc6 bxc6 9. Ba4 {The bishop stays on the a4-e8 diagonal and pins the c6 pawn.} h6 {Kicks the knight.}
10. Nf3 {White is a pawn up. Black has some activity in return, so finish developing with castling and d3.} Bd6 11. O-O O-O 12. d3 *

[Chapter "Rousseau Gambit: 3...f5"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Rousseau Gambit"]
[ECO "C50"]

{3...f5 attacks e4 and hopes for a quick attack on the f-file. Stockfish rates it more than a pawn worse for Black. The simplest answer is to decline the gambit and keep the center.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 f5?! {Attacks e4, but the f-pawn leaves f7 and opens the a2-g8 diagonal in front of Black's king.}
4. d3! {Declines the gambit. e4 is protected, and if Black takes on e4 we recapture with the pawn. Taking with 4.exf5 lets Black play ...e4 and get the open game Black is hoping for.} Nf6
(4... fxe4 5. dxe4 {Black has traded the f-pawn and opened lines toward Black's own king.} Nf6 6. Ng5! {[%cal Gg5f7,Gc4f7] The knight jumps toward f7 and e6.} d5 7. exd5 Na5 8. Bd3 {Keeps the bishop. White's pieces point at a king that has lost its f-pawn shelter.})
5. O-O {Castle first, keeping the king safe before the position opens.} Bc5 6. Nc3 d6
7. Bg5 {[%cal Gg5f6,Gc4g8] Pins the knight. Note that Black can't castle kingside: with Black's f-pawn no longer on f7, our bishop on c4 covers g8. White is clearly better.} *

[Chapter "Classical: 4.c3 Nf6 5.O-O"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "5.O-O"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Classical Variation"]
[ECO "C54"]

{White castles first and keeps both d3 and d4 in reserve. Play 5...d6, and meet 6.d4 with ...Bb6.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. O-O {Castles first, keeping both d3 and d4 in reserve.} 5... d6 {[%engine ok] Keeps our usual setup. Stockfish prefers 5...Nxe4, but after 6.d4 d5 the game turns sharp.} 6. d4 {Hits the bishop and e5 at once. White's usual move here; 6.d3 goes back to the main line (see Advanced variations).} 6... Bb6 {On b6 the bishop is safe and still aims at f2. Taking on d4 would give White a big pawn center after cxd4.} *

[Chapter "Canal Variation: 6.Bg5"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "6.Bg5"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Giuoco Pianissimo, Canal Variation"]
[ECO "C50"]

{White pins the knight on f6. Ask the bishop with ...h6, then break the pin with ...g5 before you castle, or recapture with the queen after Bxf6.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. Nc3 Nf6 5. d3 d6 6. Bg5 {This is the Canal Variation. It pins the knight.} 6... h6 7. Bh4 {The most common move, three games in four.} (7. Bxf6 Qxf6 {Black gets the bishop pair, and the queen is active on f6.} 8. Nd5 Qd8 {The queen steps back; ...c6 will kick the knight later.} 9. c3 a6) 7... g5 {[%cal Gg5h4] Breaks the pin while Black's king is still on e8. Stockfish slightly prefers Black.} 8. Bg3 a6 {Next ...Ba7, then ...Qe7 and ...Be6.} *

[Chapter "Trap: the Blackburne Shilling Gambit"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "3...Nd4"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Blackburne-Kostić Gambit"]
[ECO "C50"]

{3...Nd4 is a false gambit. It leaves e5 hanging on purpose and sets a famous trap. Take the knight, not the pawn, and watch for ...Bc5.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nd4 {Also called the Blackburne-Kostić Gambit. Leaves e5 hanging and moves the knight a second time.}
4. Nxd4 {Trades off the strong knight. White is already better: Black has moved a knight twice just to trade it.}
(4. Nxe5? {Greedy.} Qg5! {Hits the knight and g2 at once.} 5. Nxf7? Qxg2 6. Rf1 Qxe4+ 7. Be2 Nf3# {Smothered mate: White's own pieces box in the king. This is the trap Black was hoping for.})
(4. O-O {Also strong: with the king safe, Nxe5 becomes a real threat. 4.Nxd4 is a little more forceful.})
4... exd4 5. O-O {Castle first, then go after the pawn on d4.} Bc5? {Natural, but it walks into a fork.}
(5... Nf6 6. Re1 d6 7. c3 {Undermines d4.} Be7 8. cxd4 {Wins the pawn Black left on d4.} c6 9. d5 cxd5 10. exd5 O-O 11. Nc3 {A clean extra pawn and easy development.})
6. Bxf7+! {Pulls the king to f7, where Qh5+ will check it and hit the bishop on c5 at the same time.} Kxf7
7. Qh5+ {[%cal Gh5c5] Check, and the queen attacks the bishop along the fifth rank.} g6
(7... Kf8 8. Qxc5+ d6 9. Qxd4 {Two pawns up, and Black can no longer castle.})
8. Qxc5 d5 9. Qxd4 {Two pawns up with the safer king.} *

[Chapter "Trap: 4...h6 and the b-pawn push"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Classical Variation"]
[ECO "C53"]

{4...h6 stops Ng5 and Bg5, but it is too slow. The bishop on c5 and the knight on c6 are easy targets, and the b-pawn can chase them before Black has developed.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 h6?! {Prevents Ng5 and Bg5, but spends a move on a pawn while the bishop on c5 is a target.}
5. b4! {[%cal Gb4c5] Hits the bishop and gains space with tempo. Stockfish rates this as White's best try.} Bb6?!
(5... Bd6 {Black's best: the bishop stays near the center, though it blocks the d-pawn.} 6. d4 a6 7. O-O {White has a big center and easy development.})
6. b5 {[%cal Gb5c6] Chases the knight, which also has to keep guarding e5.} Na5
(6... Nce7 7. Nxe5 {With the knight gone from c6, e5 simply falls.} d5 8. exd5)
7. Bxf7+! {[%cal Gc4f7] Stronger than retreating. The bishop pulls the king out before e5 falls.} Kxf7?
(7... Kf8 {Best, but still bad.} 8. Nxe5 Qf6 9. d4 d6 10. Ng6+! {Forks the king and the rook on h8.} Kxf7 11. Nxh8+ Kf8 12. O-O {White is clearly better: Stockfish rates it about +2.0.})
8. Nxe5+ {Wins the pawn with check. The king has no good square.} Ke8??
(8... Ke7? 9. Ng6+ {Forks the king and the rook on h8.})
(8... Kf8? 9. Ng6+ {The same fork.})
(8... Ke6 9. Qh5 {Hunts the king in the open, with Qf5+ or Qf7+ coming.} Qf6 10. d4 g6 11. Qg4+ Ke7 12. Nxg6+ {Check, and the knight also hits the rook on h8.})
9. Qh5+ Ke7
(9... Kf8 10. Qf7# {Mate.})
(9... g6 10. Qxg6+ {The queen joins with check, and mate follows.})
10. Ba3+! {[%cal Ga3e7] Shuts the king's escape route before the queen lands on f7.} d6
(10... c5 11. bxc6+ {An en passant capture that uncovers check from the bishop on a3.} Bc5 12. Bxc5+ Kf6 13. Qf5# {Mate.})
11. Qf7# {Mate on move 11.} *

[Chapter "Trap: the early ...d5 and Qb3"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "5...d5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Classical Variation, Giuoco Pianissimo"]
[ECO "C54"]

{In the slow Italian, Black would love to play ...d5. Played too early, it runs into Qb3: queen and bishop line up on the a2-g8 diagonal against the knight on d5 and the f7 pawn behind it.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 d5?! {Strikes in the center before Black has castled.} 6. exd5 Nxd5
7. Qb3! {[%cal Gb3d5,Gc4f7] Hits d5 twice. If the knight retreats, the line to f7 opens.} Be6 {Defends d5, but takes the last guard away from b7.}
(7... Nf6 {Black's best: give up f7 to keep the pieces.} 8. Bxf7+ Kf8 9. O-O {White is a pawn up, and Black can no longer castle.})
(7... Nce7? 8. Qb5+! {Check, and the queen also attacks the bishop on c5.} c6 9. Qxc5 {White wins a piece.})
(7... Na5? 8. Qb5+! {Check, and the queen attacks both the knight on a5 and the bishop on c5.} c6 9. Qxc5 {White wins a piece.})
8. Qxb7 {[%cal Gb7c6] Takes the pawn and attacks the knight on c6.} Nde7 {The best defense: guard c6 with the other knight.}
9. Bxe6 fxe6 10. Qa6 {White is a clean pawn up, and Black's doubled e-pawns are weak.} *

[Chapter "Trap: the early ...Ng4"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "5...Ng4"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Classical Variation, Giuoco Pianissimo"]
[ECO "C54"]

{Black's knight jumps to g4 to attack f2. Don't defend passively: strike in the center.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 Ng4?! {[%cal Rg4f2,Rc5f2] Attacks f2 with knight and bishop.}
6. d4! {[%cal Gd4c5,Gd4e5] Hits the bishop and the e5 pawn while Black's knight is far from the center.}
(6. O-O {Also good.} Nxf2? 7. Rxf2 Bxf2+ 8. Kxf2 {Two minor pieces for a rook and pawn: a good trade for White in the middlegame.})
6... exd4
(6... Bd6 {Keeps e5, but the bishop now blocks Black's d-pawn.} 7. h3 Nf6 {The knight went to g4 and back, and the bishop blocks the d-pawn. Black has simply lost time.})
7. cxd4 Bb4+ 8. Nc3 O-O 9. O-O {Our pawns rule the center, and the knight on g4 has done nothing and must go back.} *

[Chapter "Trap: the Fishing Pole"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "7...h5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Classical Variation, Giuoco Pianissimo"]
[ECO "C54"]

{A famous trick, better known from the Ruy Lopez. Black leaves a knight on g4 as bait. If White grabs it with hxg4, the h-file opens and Black's rook and queen attack down it. The fix is simple: don't bite.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 d6 6. O-O Ng4?! {Eyes f2, but the knight is on its own.}
7. h3 {Asks the knight to leave.} h5?! {[%cal Rh8h1] The hook. If White takes on g4, ...hxg4 opens the h-file for the rook on h8.}
8. d4! {[%cal Gd4c5,Gd4e5] Ignore the bait and strike in the center. The bishop on c5 is attacked, and the knight on g4 still has to go back.}
(8. hxg4? {Takes the bait. After this, only 9.Ng5 holds; most other moves lose to the attack on the h-file.} hxg4 9. Nh2? Qh4 {Threatens ...Qxh2 mate. With the h-file open, White's king can't be defended.})
8... Bb6 9. a4 {[%cal Ga4a5] Threatens a5, when the bishop on b6 would have no squares. Black has to make room.} a6
10. Na3 {The knight heads for c4 once White's bishop moves on, to hit the bishop on b6.} Nf6
11. Bg5 {Black has lost time with the knight and weakened the kingside with ...h5. White is clearly better.} *

[Chapter "Trap: Légal's mate"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "5...Bh5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Paris Defense"]
[ECO "C50"]

{Named after the French master Sire de Légal, who is said to have won this way in Paris in 1750 (the game was more likely played around 1787). The lesson: a pinned piece can still move if the move wins more than what it uncovers.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 d6 4. Nc3 Bg4 {[%cal Rg4d1] Pins the knight on f3 to the queen on d1.}
5. h3 {Asks the bishop what it wants: take, retreat, or keep the pin.} Bh5?
(5... Bxf3 {The best answer: Black gives up the bishop to avoid tricks.} 6. Qxf3 {White has the two bishops and quick development.})
6. Nxe5! {The pinned knight moves anyway. If Black takes the queen, it's mate.} Bxd1??
(6... Nxe5 {The best way to limit the damage.} 7. Qxh5 Nxc4 8. Qb5+ {A fork: check, and the queen attacks the knight on c4.} c6 9. Qxc4 {White is a clean pawn up.})
(6... dxe5 7. Qxh5 {White has won a pawn and keeps the bishop on c4.})
7. Bxf7+ Ke7 8. Nd5# {Mate with three minor pieces. Black is a queen up and lost.} *

[Chapter "Trap: 5.Ng5 and f7"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Giuoco Pianissimo"]
[ECO "C50"]

{An early Ng5 looks scary, but once you castle, f7 is defended twice.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. d3 Nf6 5. Ng5 {[%cal Rg5f7,Rc4f7] Aims at f7 with knight and bishop.} O-O! {Castling adds the rook to f7's defenders.}
6. Nxf7? {This sacrifice doesn't work.}
(6. Nc3 h6 {Kicks the knight back.} 7. Nf3 d6 {White has spent two moves with the knight for nothing.})
6... Rxf7 7. Bxf7+ Kxf7 {Two minor pieces for a rook and pawn. Black is clearly better.} *

[Chapter "Trap: a fork trick that fails"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "5.Nxe5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Four Knights Game: Italian Variation"]
[ECO "C50"]

{After 4.Nc3 Nf6, White may try the fork trick 5.Nxe5. Here it fails, because Black's knight can take on c4 first.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. Nc3 Nf6
5. Nxe5? {Hopes for 5...Nxe5 6.d4, forking bishop and knight.} Nxe5
6. d4 Nxc4! {[%cal Ge5c4] Grab the bishop first. Now only one Black piece is attacked.}
7. dxc5
(7. Qd3? {Attacks the knight.} Bb4 8. Qxc4 Bxc3+ 9. bxc3 d5 {A piece up for a pawn, and ...d5 gains time on the queen.})
7... d5! {Opens lines while White's king is still in the center.}
8. exd5 Qe7+ {Forces the queens off, or White loses more.}
9. Qe2 Qxe2+ 10. Kxe2 Bf5 {Black has a piece for two pawns and much the better game.} *

[Chapter "Map of the variations"]
[Type "map"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 {Italian Game}
3... Bc5 {[%course white] Giuoco Piano}
(3... Nf6 {[%course white] Two Knights Defense})
(3... h6 {Anti-Fried Liver Defense})
(3... Be7 {Hungarian Defense})
